The issue in the `testCOMPRESS335` test case is related to how the TAR checksum is handled during the creation of the `ArchiveInputStream`. Here is the relevant part of the `createArchiveInputStream` method in the `ArchiveStreamFactory` class:

```java
public ArchiveInputStream createArchiveInputStream(final InputStream in) throws ArchiveException {
    // Check the signature of the input stream to determine the archive type
    // Based on the signature, create the corresponding ArchiveInputStream
    // This includes detection for ZIP, JAR, AR, CPIO, ARJ, DUMP, and TAR formats

    // Check for TAR format
    final byte[] tarheader = new byte[512];
    in.mark(tarheader.length);
    int signatureLength = IOUtils.readFully(in, tarheader);
    in.reset();

    if (TarArchiveInputStream.matches(tarheader, signatureLength)) {
        return createArchiveInputStream(TAR, in);
    }

    // Additional check for TarArchiveInputStream to verify the header checksum
    if (signatureLength >= 512) {
        TarArchiveInputStream tais = null;
        try {
            tais = new TarArchiveInputStream(new ByteArrayInputStream(tarheader));
            if (tais.getNextTarEntry().isCheckSumOK()) {
                return createArchiveInputStream(TAR, in);
            }
        } catch (Exception e) {
            // Handle exceptions and continue
        } finally {
            IOUtils.closeQuietly(tais);
        }
    }

    throw new ArchiveException("No Archiver found for the stream signature");
}
```

Based on the code snippet above, the issue may be related to how the checksum validation is performed for TAR archives. The checksum validation logic for TAR archives is triggered based on the header information read. It seems that there might be a discrepancy or issue in how the header information is processed or the checksum is validated within the `TarArchiveInputStream`.

To further investigate and potentially fix the issue, you may need to review the checksum validation logic specifically in the `TarArchiveInputStream` class or the `isCheckSumOK` method to ensure that it correctly handles cases where checksum values have zero padding on the left instead of NULL-SPACE terminator on the right.

